  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe monsoon has decided to give me a brief break tonight, as the \nheaviest showers are either in Montana or across Colorado. There \nare still a few showers around as I write this, but everything, \nat least at the time of publication, is on the light side. The \nmain show still looks to be today though, especially this\nafternoon and into tonight.\n\nThe big bowling ball, AKA the Upper Level low, continues to \nspin and slowly move eastward, getting close to the Wyoming \nborder around sunset. This will lead to another day of showers \nand thunderstorms across the area. One difference is that the \nmost numerous will likely shift to the east of the Divide, \nespecially after sunset as the low moves eastward and drier air \nmoves in behind the low. Now for the main concern, which we have\nbeen talking about for the past several days, the potential for\nheavy rain and flooding. It will still be present today, \nalthough the main threat will again be across northern Wyoming. \nPrecipitable waters of most of the guidance top out at around \nan inch across the Bighorn Basin and Johnson County, \napproaching twice of climatological normals, then gradually \ndecreasing as you head south and west. There are two areas in \nparticular that are concerning, the Cody Foothills and Absarokas\nas well as Johnson County and the eastern slopes of the Bighorn\nRange. The main concern is if the low can take a further south \ntrack, this would set up an upslope flow in these areas, \nbringing greater precipitation efficiency. The main time of \nconcern would be this afternoon and evening in the Cody \nFoothills, and later tonight and possibly into Sunday morning \nfor the Bighorns and Johnson County as the low makes it's \nclosest approach. The Weather Prediction Center agrees as well, \nas the Excessive Rainfall Outlook has been upgraded to Slight \nroughly north of a Cowley to Sussex line, with a Marginal risk \nnorth of a Lake Yellowstone to Casper Mountain line. The chance \nof flooding is less elsewhere but not zero. As for stronger \nthunderstorms, the best instability will be east of our area. \nHowever, I could see some hail with low wet bulb zero levels at \ntimes. Precipitation should finally end in western Wyoming later\ntonight as the low moves eastward.\n\nThere is one more day to get through on Sunday. The low will  \nstill be moving eastward, finally crossing into South Dakota and\nNebraska Sunday night. Some wrap around moisture will still be \naround in northern Wyoming though, especially along and east of \nthe Bighorns. The threat of heavy rain will likely linger \nanother day here, especially in the morning before gradually \ntapering off in the afternoon. Chances of showers decrease \nfurther west, with most of western and southwestern Wyoming \nbeing dry by noon on Sunday. As for the Flood Watch, we will \ncontinue it as is this morning. We may be able to trim some of \nthe western zones out for Sunday if the low moves far enough \neast, but we will cross that bridge when we get there.\n\nRidging builds over the area on Monday, bringing a nice last full \nday of astronomical summer with mild temperatures and a good deal of \nsunshine for most locations. Models are still split from later \nTuesday through Wednesday with the next potential system in the\nform of a trough moving in from the southwest. Some guidance, \nlike the ECMWF, brings it across the state bringing another \nchance of showers. Other guidance keeps it to the south with \nmainly dry conditions. The current forecast leans toward the \nwetter solution, but this is far from certain. Mainly dry \nweather then likely returns for Thursday and Friday. And, with \nlargely south to southwest flow continuing, temperatures will \nlikely average above normal through the last half of next week \nas we head into astronomical autumn, which begins Tuesday at \n6:04 pm.",
